1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Welcome to School Physics, Explained.

2
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:09,445
This is AP Physics 1, Unit 1, Kinematics,
Part 1. In this video, we'll cover five

3
00:00:09,445 --> 00:00:10,029
topics.

4
00:00:10,479 --> 00:00:13,103
Position, Displacement and Reference
Frames.

5
00:00:13,553 --> 00:00:15,857
Average and Instantaneous Velocity.

6
00:00:16,307 --> 00:00:19,720
Acceleration and Motion with Constant
Acceleration.

7
00:00:20,170 --> 00:00:22,133
Reading and Sketching Motion Graphs.

8
00:00:22,583 --> 00:00:26,167
Displacement from the Area Under a
Velocity Time Graph.

9
00:00:26,617 --> 00:00:27,833
Let's get started.

10
00:00:29,466 --> 00:00:33,519
Before we can describe motion, we have to
answer a simple question.

11
00:00:33,969 --> 00:00:38,364
Motion, compared to what? Physics starts
by choosing a reference frame.

12
00:00:39,514 --> 00:00:45,082
A reference frame is a point of view: an
origin, a positive direction, and a clock.

13
00:00:45,532 --> 00:00:50,353
Position, written x, tells us where an
object is, measured from the origin.

14
00:00:51,503 --> 00:00:53,061
Position can be negative.

15
00:00:53,511 --> 00:00:59,025
If we choose right as positive, a point
three meters to the left of the origin is

16
00:00:59,025 --> 00:01:01,340
at x equals negative three meters.

17
00:01:02,490 --> 00:01:04,879
Displacement is the change in position.

18
00:01:05,329 --> 00:01:08,593
Delta x equals x final, minus x initial.

19
00:01:09,043 --> 00:01:11,603
Its sign tells you the direction of the
change.

20
00:01:12,753 --> 00:01:18,044
A cart moves from x equals two meters, to
x equals negative four meters.

21
00:01:18,494 --> 00:01:23,721
Its displacement is negative four, minus
two, which is negative six meters.

22
00:01:24,171 --> 00:01:27,029
It moved six meters in the negative
direction.

23
00:01:28,179 --> 00:01:30,782
Now move the origin two meters to the
left.

24
00:01:31,232 --> 00:01:36,798
Every position grows by two. The cart now
goes from four meters to negative two

25
00:01:36,798 --> 00:01:37,291
meters.

26
00:01:37,741 --> 00:01:43,136
But the displacement is still negative six
meters. Displacement doesn't depend on

27
00:01:43,136 --> 00:01:47,533
where you put the origin, as long as the
axis points the same way.

28
00:01:48,683 --> 00:01:52,075
On the A P exam, always state your
coordinate system.

29
00:01:52,525 --> 00:01:57,856
Draw an arrow for the positive direction,
and keep your signs consistent from start

30
00:01:57,856 --> 00:01:58,498
to finish.

31
00:01:59,648 --> 00:02:01,120
Now try one yourself.

32
00:02:01,570 --> 00:02:07,107
A runner starts at x equals negative five
meters, runs to x equals seven meters,

33
00:02:07,107 --> 00:02:12,852
then back to x equals three meters. What
is the displacement, and what distance did

34
00:02:12,852 --> 00:02:14,029
the runner cover?

35
00:02:14,479 --> 00:02:15,759
Pause the video now.

36
00:02:16,209 --> 00:02:21,765
The displacement is three, minus negative
five, which is eight meters. The distance

37
00:02:21,765 --> 00:02:26,385
is twelve meters out, plus four meters
back, which is sixteen meters.

38
00:02:26,685 --> 00:02:31,336
A speed trap catches a car at one instant.
A road trip takes hours.

39
00:02:31,786 --> 00:02:36,394
Both involve velocity, but they ask
different questions. One is about an

40
00:02:36,394 --> 00:02:39,210
instant, and the other is about an
interval.

41
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:44,818
Average velocity is the displacement
divided by the time interval.

42
00:02:45,268 --> 00:02:50,532
On a position time graph, it's the slope
of the straight line joining two points.

43
00:02:50,532 --> 00:02:52,287
We call that a secant line.

44
00:02:53,437 --> 00:02:59,333
Here, an object is at x equals two meters
at one second, and at x equals ten meters

45
00:02:59,333 --> 00:03:00,541
at three seconds.

46
00:03:00,991 --> 00:03:06,785
The average velocity is ten minus two,
over three minus one. That's eight meters

47
00:03:06,785 --> 00:03:09,972
over two seconds, or four meters per
second.

48
00:03:11,122 --> 00:03:12,466
Now shrink the interval.

49
00:03:12,916 --> 00:03:18,106
As the second point slides toward the
first, the secant line turns, and settles

50
00:03:18,106 --> 00:03:19,551
onto the tangent line.

51
00:03:20,001 --> 00:03:25,451
The slope of the tangent line is the
instantaneous velocity: the velocity at

52
00:03:25,451 --> 00:03:26,742
that exact moment.

53
00:03:27,892 --> 00:03:33,426
So on any position time graph, read the
instantaneous velocity from the steepness

54
00:03:33,426 --> 00:03:34,314
of the curve.

55
00:03:34,764 --> 00:03:40,387
Where the graph is steepest, the object is
fastest. Where the tangent is flat, the

56
00:03:40,387 --> 00:03:42,444
object is momentarily at rest.

57
00:03:43,594 --> 00:03:46,986
Speed is the size of the velocity, with no
direction.

58
00:03:47,436 --> 00:03:53,136
An object moving at negative five meters
per second has a speed of five meters per

59
00:03:53,136 --> 00:03:53,622
second.

60
00:03:54,772 --> 00:03:56,244
Now try one yourself.

61
00:03:56,694 --> 00:04:02,885
An object is at x equals negative three
meters at time zero, and at x equals nine

62
00:04:02,885 --> 00:04:07,318
meters at time four seconds. What is its
average velocity?

63
00:04:07,768 --> 00:04:09,048
Pause the video now.

64
00:04:09,498 --> 00:04:15,004
The displacement is nine minus negative
three, which is twelve meters. Divide by

65
00:04:15,004 --> 00:04:19,546
four seconds, and the average velocity is
three meters per second.

66
00:04:19,846 --> 00:04:25,429
Velocity tells us how position changes.
Acceleration tells us how velocity

67
00:04:25,429 --> 00:04:26,033
changes.

68
00:04:26,483 --> 00:04:31,649
In this lesson, we'll define acceleration
carefully, and build the equations for

69
00:04:31,649 --> 00:04:33,587
motion when it stays constant.

70
00:04:34,737 --> 00:04:40,283
Average acceleration is the change in
velocity, divided by the time interval.

71
00:04:40,733 --> 00:04:46,373
On a velocity time graph, it's the slope.
A straight line means the acceleration is

72
00:04:46,373 --> 00:04:46,984
constant.

73
00:04:48,134 --> 00:04:54,222
Watch the signs. Acceleration has the sign
of the change in velocity, not the sign of

74
00:04:54,222 --> 00:04:55,153
the velocity.

75
00:04:55,603 --> 00:05:01,234
An object moving in the negative direction
and speeding up has a negative velocity,

76
00:05:01,234 --> 00:05:03,133
and a negative acceleration.

77
00:05:03,583 --> 00:05:09,455
An object moving in the negative direction
and slowing down has a negative velocity,

78
00:05:09,455 --> 00:05:11,413
but a positive acceleration.

79
00:05:12,563 --> 00:05:17,939
For constant acceleration, three equations
connect the five kinematic quantities.

80
00:05:18,389 --> 00:05:24,938
v equals v naught plus a t. Delta x equals
v naught t, plus one half a t squared. And

81
00:05:24,938 --> 00:05:29,098
v squared equals v naught squared, plus
two a delta x.

82
00:05:29,548 --> 00:05:35,557
A fourth one is also handy: delta x equals
the average of the initial and final

83
00:05:35,557 --> 00:05:37,079
velocities, times t.

84
00:05:38,229 --> 00:05:44,012
Here's an A P style example. A cart rolls
up a ramp at four meters per second, and

85
00:05:44,012 --> 00:05:46,762
slows at two meters per second squared.

86
00:05:47,212 --> 00:05:50,220
When does it stop, and how far up the ramp
does it get?

87
00:05:50,670 --> 00:05:56,731
Take up the ramp as positive, so a is
negative two. It stops when v is zero:

88
00:05:56,731 --> 00:06:00,718
zero equals four minus two t, so t is two
seconds.

89
00:06:01,168 --> 00:06:06,393
The distance is the average velocity, two
meters per second, times two seconds,

90
00:06:06,393 --> 00:06:07,781
which is four meters.

91
00:06:08,931 --> 00:06:10,403
Now try one yourself.

92
00:06:10,853 --> 00:06:16,233
A car moving at twenty meters per second
brakes with an acceleration of negative

93
00:06:16,233 --> 00:06:21,008
five meters per second squared. How far
does it travel before stopping?

94
00:06:21,458 --> 00:06:22,738
Pause the video now.

95
00:06:23,188 --> 00:06:29,886
Use v squared equals v naught squared plus
two a delta x. Zero equals four hundred,

96
00:06:29,886 --> 00:06:33,599
minus ten delta x. So delta x is forty
meters.

97
00:06:33,899 --> 00:06:38,715
On the A P exam, you'll often get one
motion graph, and be asked to sketch the

98
00:06:38,715 --> 00:06:39,147
others.

99
00:06:39,597 --> 00:06:44,482
Here's how to translate between position,
velocity and acceleration graphs.

100
00:06:45,632 --> 00:06:46,891
Start with position.

101
00:06:47,341 --> 00:06:52,373
The slope of the position graph at each
moment gives the value of the velocity

102
00:06:52,373 --> 00:06:52,760
graph.

103
00:06:53,910 --> 00:06:55,019
Then do it again.

104
00:06:55,469 --> 00:07:00,184
The slope of the velocity graph gives the
value of the acceleration graph.

105
00:07:01,334 --> 00:07:06,548
Let's try one. A cart moves forward at a
steady speed, stops for a while, then

106
00:07:06,548 --> 00:07:08,352
moves backward more slowly.

107
00:07:08,802 --> 00:07:13,560
The position graph is three straight
pieces: rising, flat, then falling.

108
00:07:14,710 --> 00:07:19,843
So the velocity graph is three flat
pieces: positive, then zero, then

109
00:07:19,843 --> 00:07:20,512
negative.

110
00:07:20,962 --> 00:07:25,950
Each velocity piece is flat, so the
acceleration is zero, except at the

111
00:07:25,950 --> 00:07:29,112
instants where the velocity suddenly
changes.

112
00:07:30,262 --> 00:07:32,758
Now a ball rolls up a ramp, and back down.

113
00:07:33,208 --> 00:07:38,417
Its velocity graph is a straight line
sloping down, crossing zero at the top of

114
00:07:38,417 --> 00:07:39,010
the ramp.

115
00:07:39,460 --> 00:07:43,194
So the acceleration graph is a constant
negative value.

116
00:07:43,644 --> 00:07:48,963
And the position graph is a curve that
opens downward, peaking exactly when the

117
00:07:48,963 --> 00:07:50,108
velocity is zero.

118
00:07:51,258 --> 00:07:53,050
Use these checks every time.

119
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:58,741
Where the velocity is zero, the position
graph is flat. Where the velocity is

120
00:07:58,741 --> 00:08:00,988
positive, the position is rising.

121
00:08:01,438 --> 00:08:06,024
And a curved position graph always means
the acceleration isn't zero.

122
00:08:07,174 --> 00:08:08,646
Now try one yourself.

123
00:08:09,096 --> 00:08:14,871
A velocity graph is a horizontal line at
negative three meters per second. Describe

124
00:08:14,871 --> 00:08:18,142
the position graph, and the acceleration
graph.

125
00:08:18,592 --> 00:08:19,872
Pause the video now.

126
00:08:20,322 --> 00:08:25,450
The position graph is a straight line
sloping down, with a slope of negative

127
00:08:25,450 --> 00:08:30,242
three meters per second. The acceleration
graph is zero the whole time.

128
00:08:30,542 --> 00:08:34,105
The slope of a velocity time graph gives
acceleration.

129
00:08:34,555 --> 00:08:38,565
The area under it gives something just as
useful: the displacement.

130
00:08:39,715 --> 00:08:45,024
Why does this work? For a short time
interval, displacement is velocity times

131
00:08:45,024 --> 00:08:45,369
time.

132
00:08:45,819 --> 00:08:48,528
On the graph, that's the area of a thin
rectangle.

133
00:08:48,978 --> 00:08:53,860
Add up all the thin rectangles, and you
get the total area, which is the total

134
00:08:53,860 --> 00:08:54,674
displacement.

135
00:08:55,824 --> 00:08:58,683
Area below the time axis counts as
negative.

136
00:08:59,133 --> 00:09:02,674
It means the object was moving in the
negative direction.

137
00:09:03,124 --> 00:09:07,945
So the displacement is the area above the
axis, minus the area below.

138
00:09:09,095 --> 00:09:14,359
Here's an example. An object speeds up
from zero to eight meters per second in

139
00:09:14,359 --> 00:09:18,205
four seconds, then holds that speed for
two more seconds.

140
00:09:18,655 --> 00:09:23,828
The first part is a triangle: one half
times four times eight, which is sixteen

141
00:09:23,828 --> 00:09:24,287
meters.

142
00:09:24,737 --> 00:09:29,174
The second part is a rectangle: two times
eight, which is sixteen meters.

143
00:09:29,624 --> 00:09:32,248
The total displacement is thirty two
meters.

144
00:09:33,398 --> 00:09:39,010
If the question asks for distance instead
of displacement, add the sizes of all the

145
00:09:39,010 --> 00:09:42,187
areas, treating the negative areas as
positive.

146
00:09:43,337 --> 00:09:44,809
Now try one yourself.

147
00:09:45,259 --> 00:09:50,607
A velocity graph goes from six meters per
second down to negative two meters per

148
00:09:50,607 --> 00:09:55,888
second, in a straight line over four
seconds. It crosses zero at three seconds.

149
00:09:55,888 --> 00:09:59,297
What is the displacement, and what is the
distance?

150
00:09:59,747 --> 00:10:01,027
Pause the video now.

151
00:10:01,477 --> 00:10:06,639
The triangle above the axis is one half
times three times six, which is nine

152
00:10:06,639 --> 00:10:12,005
meters. The triangle below is one half
times one times two, which is one meter.

153
00:10:12,005 --> 00:10:16,623
So the displacement is eight meters, and
the distance is ten meters.

154
00:10:17,073 --> 00:10:20,572
Next time, we'll apply all of this to free
fall problems.

155
00:10:21,722 --> 00:10:26,557
If this lesson helped, subscribe to School
Physics, Explained, and tap the bell so

156
00:10:26,557 --> 00:10:28,207
you don't miss the next one.

